# Java switch selection and delegation

## Rules

- **CORP-JAVA-SW-001.1 (SHOULD):** Prefer `switch` when selecting among multiple cases of the same discriminator; retain `if`/`if-else` for binary decisions.
- **CORP-JAVA-SW-001.2 (SHOULD):** A `case` SHOULD dispatch to a cohesive operation, not contain a substantial implementation.
- **CORP-JAVA-SW-001.3 (MAY):** Trivial, immediate operations MAY be written directly within a `case` (e.g. one assignment, direct return or short method call).
- **CORP-JAVA-SW-001.4 (MUST):** Avoid extended, nested or multi-stage business logic inside `case` arms. Extract a named method instead.
- **CORP-JAVA-SW-001.5 (MUST):** The selected alternative must preserve existing behavior, including handling of unsupported values.

## Example: preferred

```java
switch (action) {
    case "build" -> buildProject();
    case "test" -> runTests();
    case "release" -> prepareRelease();
    default -> reportUnknownAction(action);
}
```

## Example: avoid implementing a full process inside the case

```java
switch (action) {
    case "build" -> {
        loadModules();
        validateModules();
        compileModules();
        writeBuildReport();
    }
    default -> reportUnknownAction(action);
}
```

A one-line `return` or assignment inside a case is acceptable when its intent is immediately clear. `switch` statements and expressions may both be used when appropriate.
